  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1251 AM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nAn upper-level low will move west to east across the CWA throughout \nthe day Sunday. The upper-level jet on the backside of this system \nwill strengthen Sunday evening into Sunday night as it dives \nsoutheastward into the Central Plains. A slight intensification of \nthe 700mb winds will be possible during this time, though the 700mb \njet remains fairly weak. 700mb temperatures cool into the single \ndigits with this low, bringing in a nice cool down with high \ntemperatures in the 60s and 70s. The cold front will bring \nwidespread precipitation chances to the region as moisture will be \nquite good with this system, with precipitable water values in the \n90th percentile per NAEFS guidance. A cool, rainy day will certainly \nstart bringing the fall vibes back to southeast Wyoming and western \nNebraska. These fall vibes will continue for Monday as temperatures \nremain quite cool behind the system. Additionally, winds will turn \neasterly as a high strengthens over Iowa and Minnesota. Stout, \neasterly flow will result in continued upslope develop throughout \nthe day Monday before winds turn back southerly for Tuesday.\n\nTuesday onwards, the upper-level pattern will remain quite \nprogressive with multiple systems moving through the region. Daily \nprecipitation chances will continue through much of the week. \nTuesday will start off a warming trend across the region, with highs \nin the low- to upper-70s, followed by low-70s to low-80s Wednesday, \nand mid-70s to mid-80s Thursday and Friday. Luckily, winds look to \nremain fairly calm despite the systems moving through the region. \nMost of the systems will be fairly weak, with limited 700mb jet \nstrength.",
